WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:13.980
Take an invented population of 100 individuals, with a per capita growth rate of 0.2 per day. After 10 days, you might expect 300.

2
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.480
If nothing stops it, there are about 739.

3
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.760
Growth compounds. Each new individual starts adding individuals of its own.

4
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:31.850
Call the number of individuals N. Call the per capita rate r.

5
00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:39.070
Growth each day is r times N, so more individuals add more.

6
00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.610
A constant r gives a constant doubling time.

7
00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:50.770
Here it is about 3.47 days, whether the population holds 100 or 100,000.

8
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.300
Real populations meet a brake.

9
00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:01.480
Each individual typically gets less food, space or shelter, so births fall or deaths

10
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:06.260
rise. The logistic model puts the brake on the per capita rate.

11
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.760
That rate falls in a straight line, from r when N is near zero, to zero

12
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.320
at K. K is the carrying capacity.

13
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:24.553
Total growth is that per capita rate times N. At low N, there are few

14
00:01:24.553 --> 00:01:25.813
individuals to multiply.

15
00:01:26.673 --> 00:01:29.433
Near K, each one adds almost nothing.

16
00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:33.953
So the product is greatest in the middle, at K over 2.

17
00:01:34.693 --> 00:01:36.873
There it is 50 individuals per day.

18
00:01:37.666 --> 00:01:41.146
Now run both models from a hundred individuals.

19
00:01:42.066 --> 00:01:45.966
At day 10, the exponential model gives 739.

20
00:01:46.746 --> 00:01:49.366
The logistic model gives about 451.

21
00:01:50.466 --> 00:01:52.586
They agree at first, then part.

22
00:01:53.346 --> 00:02:01.026
By day 20, the exponential model predicts 5,460, and the logistic about 858.

23
00:02:01.886 --> 00:02:05.246
These numbers are illustrative, not measured for any species.

24
00:02:06.216 --> 00:02:09.996
K is an equilibrium, not a ceiling.

25
00:02:10.736 --> 00:02:16.616
If N starts above K, the model predicts decline back toward it.

26
00:02:16.996 --> 00:02:22.916
If N starts below, the continuous model rises smoothly and never overshoots.

27
00:02:23.976 --> 00:02:29.376
Real populations have age structure, time lags and a K that changes.

28
00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:32.016
Any of these can produce overshoot.

29
00:02:32.016 --> 00:02:37.536
Keep the rule: the per capita rate falls as N rises.
